\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>45\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 51\u00b0F, and the record is 72\u00b0F (1909, 1989). The low of <strong>37\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Cleveland has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Cleveland's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Cleveland has set <strong class=\"sig\">27 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 63 that chance alone would give, and <strong>80 record highs</strong>, more than the 63 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (50). Cleveland's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on June 25, 1988.","t_chg":"Cleveland's days and nights both warmed. Days warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 155 complete years: Cleveland's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.06\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +0.13), about +1.0\u00b0F since 1871. Cleveland's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.25 to +0.36), about +4.7\u00b0F since 1871.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Cleveland has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Cleveland's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.32 to +0.78), about +8.5 inches since 1871. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 2011 (65.32 in); the driest, 1963 (18.63 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}